speechtext:
Hon. gentlemen opposite knew that there was an order in council which governed this matter. I have in my hand order in council, P.C. 1639, approved July 19, 1920, at a time when the Right Hon. Arthur Meighen, head of the previous Conservative administration, was Prime Minister of this country. This order in council was passed upon the recommendation of the Right Hon. Arthur Meighen. It deals with the functions of the Prime Minister, with the question of quorum, with the dissolution and convocation of parliament, and in connection with appointments, it reads:
Privy Councillors;
Cabinet Ministers;
Lieutenant-Governors;
Provincial Administrators;
Speaker of the Senate;

Beauharnois Power-Report
Chief Justices of all courts;
Senators;
Sub-committees of council;
Treasury Board;
Committee of Internal Eeonomjr, House of Commons;
Deputy heads of departments;
Librarians of parliament;
Crown appointments in both houses of parliament;
Governor General's secretary's staff.
This order in council made it a function of the Prime Minister to appoint deputy ministers. I must say that when I came into office I had no knowledge of that particular order in council, and I permitted my colleagues who were ministers of departments to make their own recommendation to the cabinet. This particular order was brought to my attention by the Clerk of the Privy Council after we had been in office some years, and after that time I held still to the practice of permitting my colleagues to choose whom they wished as their deputy ministers, I making the appointment as was my duty in accordance with the order in council of the day.' To show that I alone in the case of Mr. Henry, was not the only one to make the appointment of a deputy minister, I will quote from an order in council passed July 14, 1919. This particular order was passed at a time when Mr. Meighen was Prime Minister. It reads:
The committee of the privy council on the recommendation of the president of the privy council advise that Lieut.-Col. John A. Amyot, M.B., C.M.G., of the city of Toronto, be appointed Deputy Minister of Health under the provisions of the act passed at the recent session of parliament, intituled "An act respecting the Department of Health."
Mr. Meighen was president of the Council.
Does any hon. member suppose that Mr. Meighen had any ulterior motive in making that particular appointment? He was just carryin out the provisions of his own order in council with respect to the powers of the Prime Minister.
